<<<
2. Had in parallel inverted clauses. Had we desired twenty-seven amendments, got seven accepted, & were in anticipation of favourable decisions in the other twenty cases, we should think ... To write Had we desired & were in anticipation is wrong (see Evuipsis 6);
===
**2.** *Had* in parallel inverted clauses. *Had we desired twenty-seven amendments, got seven accepted, & were in anticipation of favourable decisions in the other twenty cases, we should think* . . . To write *Had we desired & were in anticipation* is wrong (see ^^ELLIPSIS^^ 6);
>>>

<<<
to write Had we desired & were we in anticipation, though legitimate, is not only heavily formal, but also slightly misleading, because it suggests two separate conditions whereas there is only a single compound one.
===
to write *Had we desired & were we in anticipation*, though legitimate, is not only heavily formal, but also slightly misleading, because it suggests two separate conditions whereas there is only a single compound one.
>>>

<<<
This common difficulty is best met by avoiding the inversion when there are parallel clauses; write here If we had desired & were in anticipation.
===
This common difficulty is best met by avoiding the inversion when there are parallel clauses; write here *If we had desired & were in anticipation*.
>>>

